%I A398710 #27 Aug 16 2026 18:34:16
%S A398710 113,117,119,131,137,171,173,179,197,231,237,297,311,313,317,319,371,
%T A398710 373,379,411,413,417,419,431,437,471,473,479,531,537,597,611,613,617,
%U A398710 619,671,673,679,711,713,717,719,731,737,797,831,837,897,971,973,979,1131,1137
%N A398710 Numbers > 100 such that all the substrings of length = 2 are primes and are all distinct.
%C A398710 The sequence is finite, with last term a(6296) = 619737131179 (12 digits).
%C A398710 Differs from A211683, where the substrings need not be distinct: a(1) = 113 while A211683(1) = 111, with the next difference being a(52) = 1131 while A211683(53) = 1111.
%C A398710 There are 21 two-digit primes. Eleven of them can occur only as the first 2-digit substring of a term, namely 23, 29, 41, 43, 47, 53, 59, 61, 67, 83 and 89, since they begin with 5 or an even digit. The remaining ten, namely 11, 13, 17, 19, 31, 37, 71, 73, 79 and 97, can occur at any position.
%C A398710 This sequence is naturally interpreted as the set of all directed trails of length at least 2 in the graph whose vertices are the decimal digits and whose edges are the two-digit primes. The longest trails have 11 edges (hence 12 digits) and there are 192 such 12-digit terms all ending with 9 (see Diophante link for proof).
%C A398710 For k = 3, 4, ..., 12, the number of terms with k digits is respectively 51, 126, 264, 500, 809, 1144, 1322, 1172, 716, and 192.
%H A398710 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A398710/b398710.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..6296</a>
%H A398710 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/h-graphes-et-circuits/6660-h178-le-safari-des-premiers">H178 = Le safari des premiers</a> (in French).
%t A398710 q[k_] := Module[{s = FromDigits /@ Partition[IntegerDigits[k], 2, 1]}, UnsameQ @@ s && AllTrue[s, PrimeQ]]; Select[Range[101, 1200], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 07 2026 *)
%o A398710 (Python)
%o A398710 from sympy import primerange
%o A398710 def afull(): # returns the full sequence
%o A398710     primes = [str(p) for p in primerange(11, 100)]
%o A398710     neighs = {p:set(q for q in primes if q[0] == p[1]) for p in primes}
%o A398710     expand, reach = [(p,) for p in primes], set()
%o A398710     while expand:
%o A398710         t = expand.pop()
%o A398710         used = set(t)
%o A398710         if len(t) > 1:
%o A398710             reach.add(t)
%o A398710         for q in neighs[t[-1]]:
%o A398710             if q not in used:
%o A398710                 expand.append(t+(q,))
%o A398710     return sorted(int("".join(q[i][0] for i in range(len(q)-1))+q[-1]) for q in reach)
%o A398710 print(afull()[:53]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 07 2026
%Y A398710 Cf. A019546, A035232, A211681, A397784.
%Y A398710 Subsequence of A211683.
